<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- TestEmpty.world plus one walking human actor for Proximity Stop /
     Adaptive Separation trials (paper 2, docs/paper2_draft.tex sec 4/7).
     The robot's own control path detects this actor via lidar (/scan),
     not ground truth - see proximity_stop.py / adaptive_separation.py.
     Ground-truth pose (this actor's own OdometryPublisher, below) is
     published for trial instrumentation/logging only, never read by the
     control path; an earlier version of this comment described ground
     truth as the detection mechanism itself, which stopped being true
     once Proximity Stop moved to lidar-based detection and was never
     updated here - fixed now, not a design change.

     First cut of this world (superseded, keeping the reasoning here since
     it's a real dead end worth not repeating) used a primitive
     cylinder+sphere body pose-driven at runtime via Gazebo's
     /world/<world>/set_pose service, on the wrong assumption that this
     container has no internet access to fetch a proper actor mesh from
     Fuel. That assumption was wrong (confirmed: `curl
     https://fuel.gazebosim.org/...` succeeds from inside sara_jazzy) and
     the primitive-body approach was a real downgrade: set_pose's
     round-trip latency caps updates at ~3.3Hz (choppy motion), and it
     doesn't use gz-sim's own trajectory system at all.

     RE-DERIVED 2026-09-25, three times, the last a design change not just a
     number change. Attempt 1: closest approach 0.6m, designed around the
     framework's original 1.2m zone radius; no longer reliably enters the
     zone once it tightened to 0.3m (footprint-grounded, see
     proximity_stop.py). Attempt 2: moved to x=0.15 - too close: the
     robot's own physical body (base_platform's collision box) extends
     forward to about x=0.076m at spawn, and a full walking-human skinned
     mesh (Mingfei/actor from Fuel, gz-sim's own actor tutorial model) has
     real leg width and swing - at this distance its legs visually passed
     through the robot's own body, confirmed by direct observation in
     Gazebo (no physics collision on the actor, so nothing stopped it).
     Attempt 3: moved to x=0.20 - still overlapped. The actual problem
     wasn't the specific distance, it was the mesh: a walking human's legs
     occupy roughly ground-to-hip height, the same height range as the
     robot's own low cart structure, so at any distance close enough to
     register in a 0.3m near-contact zone, a full-body mesh with limb
     articulation and no collision will visually intersect the robot to
     some degree - there is no single "right" distance for the skinned
     mesh case, confirmed by direct observation. Replaced the actor's skin/
     animation with a plain cylinder link instead: same native gz-sim
     <actor> element, same <script><trajectory> waypoint system, smooth
     motion with no external polling (still avoiding the choppy
     runtime-set_pose approach rejected above) - but geometrically exact
     and simple to reason about instead of an articulated mesh silhouette
     that varies frame to frame. Closest approach x=0.33 (cylinder radius
     0.15m, so its near edge sits at x=0.18): 0.104m of clearance from the
     robot's own front edge (0.076m) with zero ambiguity about limb
     position, while its near edge is still 0.244m from the lidar -
     comfortably inside the 0.3m zone (0.056m margin) and 0.044m above the
     0.2m sensor floor, not relying on any animation-stride luck.

     RE-DERIVED 2026-09-25 a fourth time, a mechanism change not just a
     geometry change. Giving the cylinder+sphere real collision geometry
     (previous attempt, still described above for the reasoning) did NOT
     stop it visually passing through the robot - confirmed empirically
     (robot's own /odom stayed at exactly (0,0,0) throughout, so no contact
     force was ever actually applied to anything). Root cause: gz-sim's
     <actor> entity type is kinematic by design - its pose is set directly
     from <script><trajectory> waypoint interpolation every timestep,
     regardless of what the physics engine computes, collision geometry or
     not. This is inherent to the Actor entity type (built for scripted/
     skeletal-animated characters), not a mistake in this file's SDF.
     Replaced the <actor> with a real <model> instead (body cylinder + head
     sphere on one link, same dimensions as the rejected actor attempt,
     properly combined into a single rigid body with one inertial instead
     of two separate links), driven by gz-sim's own documented
     `gz-sim-velocity-control-system` plugin
     (https://gazebosim.org/api/gazebo/6/classignition_1_1gazebo_1_1systems_1_1VelocityControl.html) -
     a standard, continuous per-timestep velocity controller for a model
     (not position-polling, so it keeps the smooth motion the rejected
     runtime-set_pose approach lacked), and because it's a genuine physics-
     integrated model this time, contact with the robot produces real
     collision response. Driven externally by a small ROS 2 node
     (`sara_safety/trials/run_proximity_trial.py` or a dedicated script)
     publishing alternating forward/backward velocity on the bridged
     `/model/human_actor/cmd_vel` topic - see proximity_trial.launch.py's
     bridge_cmd for the bridge entry. Because contact is now physically
     enforced rather than precomputed, the drive script does not need to
     time a precise stop distance the way the waypoint trajectory did -
     it can drive toward the robot and let physics arrest it at actual
     contact, which is the whole point of switching to a dynamic model. -->

<sdf version='1.7'>
  <world name='default'>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"></plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system" name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"></plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system" name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"></plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Imu"></plugin>

    <light name='sun' type='directional'>
      <cast_shadows>1</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 -0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>1000</range>
        <constant>0.9</constant>
        <linear>0.01</linear>
        <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>
    </light>

    <model name='ground_plane'>
      <static>1</static>
      <link name='link'>
        <collision name='collision'>
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>100 100</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction>
              <ode>
                <mu>100</mu>
                <mu2>50</mu2>
              </ode>
            </friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name='visual'>
          <cast_shadows>0</cast_shadows>
          <geometry>
            <plane>
              <normal>0 0 1</normal>
              <size>100 100</size>
            </plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.01 0.01 0.01 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- RE-DERIVED 2026-09-25, cylinder+ball, final approach. Two real
         gz-sim bugs were found and fixed along the way, both worth
         keeping on record since they'd bite again otherwise:
         (1) A skinned actor's <trajectory> defaults to tension=0, a
         Catmull-Rom spline Gazebo's own docs
         (https://gazebosim.org/docs/harmonic/actors/) explicitly say
         "may cause the animation to overshoot waypoints" - confirmed
         exactly: observed overshooting both the near (0.33) and far
         (3.0) waypoints. tension=1 fixes this ("will cause the animation
         to stick to the waypoints").
         (2) A plain-geometry <actor> (no <skin>/<animation> at all)
         cannot use <script><trajectory> in gz-sim at all - confirmed via
         a filed gz-sim issue (gazebosim/gz-sim#1932) hitting the exact
         same "Actor skin mesh not found" warning seen here, and matching
         upstream commentary that trajectory playback for non-skeletal
         actors was a Gazebo Classic feature never carried into gz-sim.
         So the cylinder+ball shape genuinely cannot be an <actor> at all
         in this Gazebo version - not a configuration mistake, a real gap.

         Given that, this is a plain <model> (not an actor), moved by
         directly, repeatedly setting its pose via /world/default/set_pose
         from an external script. This was tried once already and looked
         broken (pose data updated correctly but the GUI never visually
         moved) - root-caused via a second filed gz-sim issue
         (gazebosim/gz-sim#1403): repeated set_pose calls faster than
         roughly 5-10Hz overwhelm the GUI's SceneBroadcaster
         deserialization, which then stalls permanently rather than
         catching up. Fixed by explicitly pacing the driver script's calls
         well under that threshold (see sara_safety/trials/ driver
         script - at least ~0.3-0.5s between individual set_pose calls,
         not back-to-back). Closest approach: pivot x=0.33, cylinder
         radius 0.15m, so the near edge is at x=0.18 (0.33-0.15) - 0.104m
         of clearance from the robot's verified front edge (0.076m, from
         the actual STL mesh bounds) and 0.244m from the lidar (inside
         the 0.3m zone, 0.056m margin). Verify via an actual screenshot,
         not just topic data - that specific assumption has already been
         wrong once for this exact mechanism. -->
    <actor name='human_actor'>
      <!-- Plain cylinder link, no skin/animation - smooth native actor
           trajectory interpolation (not set_pose polling), no collision,
           no physics. tension='1' below fixes the overshoot bug
           confirmed earlier (default tension=0 is a Catmull-Rom spline
           that overshoots waypoints). Testing directly whether the
           earlier "requires a skin" conclusion was actually wrong - the
           "Actor skin mesh not found" warning may be harmless (an
           internal fallback-skin lookup, unrelated to trajectory
           interpolation) rather than proof trajectory playback needs one. -->
      <link name='link'>
        <visual name='body_visual'>
          <geometry>
            <cylinder><radius>0.15</radius><length>1.5</length></cylinder>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.8 0.4 0.1 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.4 0.1 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <visual name='head_visual'>
          <pose>0 0 0.87 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><sphere><radius>0.12</radius></sphere></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.8 0.6 0.4 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.6 0.4 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
      <script>
        <loop>true</loop>
        <delay_start>0.0</delay_start>
        <auto_start>true</auto_start>
        <trajectory id='0' type='walk' tension='1'>
          <waypoint><time>0.000</time><pose>3.0 0 0.75 0 0 0</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>1.907</time><pose>0.33 0 0.75 0 0 0</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>2.907</time><pose>0.33 0 0.75 0 0 0</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>4.814</time><pose>3.0 0 0.75 0 0 0</pose></waypoint>
          <waypoint><time>5.814</time><pose>3.0 0 0.75 0 0 0</pose></waypoint>
        </trajectory>
      </script>
    </actor>

    <gravity>0 0 -9.8</gravity>
    <magnetic_field>6e-06 2.3e-05 -4.2e-05</magnetic_field>
    <atmosphere type='adiabatic'/>
    <!-- type='ode' here is a no-op, inherited from before this session -
         gz-sim always runs dartsim regardless of this attribute. Do not
         describe SARA's simulated physics as ODE anywhere (paper included)
         on the strength of this tag. -->
    <physics type='ode'>
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1</real_time_factor>
      <real_time_update_rate>1000</real_time_update_rate>
    </physics>
    <scene>
      <ambient>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</ambient>
      <background>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</background>
      <shadows>1</shadows>
    </scene>
    <spherical_coordinates>
      <surface_model>EARTH_WGS84</surface_model>
      <latitude_deg>0</latitude_deg>
      <longitude_deg>0</longitude_deg>
      <elevation>0</elevation>
      <heading_deg>0</heading_deg>
    </spherical_coordinates>
  </world>
</sdf>
